Let's build a full-fledged API in PHP. We'll create a RESTful API that allows users to perform CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations on a collection of items. We'll use the Laravel framework to make things easier.

First, let's set up a new Laravel project. Run the following commands in your terminal:

```bash
composer create-project --prefer-dist laravel/laravel api-project
cd api-project
composer require laravel/passport
php artisan key:generate
```

Next, let's configure the database settings in the `.env` file. Update the following values:

```bash
DB_CONNECTION=mysql
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=3306
DB_DATABASE=api_project
DB_USERNAME=root
DB_PASSWORD=
```

Now, let's create a new migration to create the `items` table. Run the following command:

```bash
php artisan make:migration create_items_table
```

Open the newly created migration file (`database/migrations/*_create_items_table.php`) and add the following code:

```php
use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

class CreateItemsTable extends Migration
{
    public function up()
    {
        Schema::create('items', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->id();
            $table->string('name');
            $table->string('description');
            $table->timestamps();
        });
    }

    public function down()
    {
        Schema::dropIfExists('items');
    }
}
```

Run the following command to execute the migration:

```bash
php artisan migrate
```

Next, let's create a new model for the `items` table. Run the following command:

```bash
php artisan make:model Item
```

Open the newly created model file (`app/Models/Item.php`) and add the following code:

```php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Factories\HasFactory;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Item extends Model
{
    use HasFactory;

    protected $fillable = [
        'name',
        'description',
    ];
}
```

Now, let's create a new API controller to handle the CRUD operations. Run the following command:

```bash
php artisan make:controller API/ItemController --api
```

Open the newly created controller file (`app/Http/Controllers/API/ItemController.php`) and add the following code:

```php
namespace App\Http\Controllers\API;

use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use App\Models\Item;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Validator;

class ItemController extends Controller
{
    public function index()
    {
        $items = Item::all();
        return response()->json($items);
    }

    public function store(Request $request)
    {
        $validator = Validator::make($request->all(), [
            'name' => 'required',
            'description' => 'required',
        ]);

        if ($validator->fails()) {
            return response()->json($validator->errors(), 422);
        }

        $item = new Item();
        $item->name = $request->input('name');
        $item->description = $request->input('description');
        $item->save();

        return response()->json(['message' => 'Item created successfully'], 201);
    }

    public function show($id)
    {
        $item = Item::find($id);
        if (!$item) {
            return response()->json(['message' => 'Item not found'], 404);
        }
        return response()->json($item);
    }

    public function update(Request $request, $id)
    {
        $validator = Validator::make($request->all(), [
            'name' => 'required',
            'description' => 'required',
        ]);

        if ($validator->fails()) {
            return response()->json($validator->errors(), 422);
        }

        $item = Item::find($id);
        if (!$item) {
            return response()->json(['message' => 'Item not found'], 404);
        }

        $item->name = $request->input('name');
        $item->description = $request->input('description');
        $item->save();

        return response()->json(['message' => 'Item updated successfully']);
    }

    public function destroy($id)
    {
        $item = Item::find($id);
        if (!$item) {
            return response()->json(['message' => 'Item not found'], 404);
        }
        $item->delete();
        return response()->json(['message' => 'Item deleted successfully']);
    }
}
```

Finally, let's add a new route for the API controller. Open the `routes/api.php` file and add the following code:

```php
Route::apiResource('items', API\ItemController::class);
```

That's it! You now have a fully functional API with CRUD operations on a collection of items. To test the API, you can use a tool like Postman or cURL. Here are some examples:

* GET `http://localhost:8000/api/items`: Retrieves a list of all items.
* POST `http://localhost:8000/api/items`: Creates a new item. Send a JSON payload with the required fields (`name` and `description`).
* GET `http://localhost:8000/api/items/{id}`: Retrieves a single item by ID.
* PUT `http://localhost:8000/api/items/{id}`: Updates an existing item. Send a JSON payload with the required fields (`name` and `description`).
* DELETE `http://localhost:8000/api/items/{id}`: Deletes an existing item.

